    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nFord Gets Into the Spacecraft Manufacturing Business by Buying Into Revolution Space\n6 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nI remember a time, not long ago, when American carmaker Ford rolled out so many new vehicles that we, the ones covering such things, sometimes found it difficult to keep up. That time is gone now, and we all have to think pretty hard to remember how long ago a brand-new Ford came out.\nIn a bid to cut costs and boost profit margins, the Blue Oval decided to stick to its present range of trucks and SUVs, with the only major investment on the horizon being the upcoming Fathom family. That, and a spacecraft, it seems.\nHaving determined through undisclosed internal mechanisms that the space industry is the way of the future, the carmaker announced this week it has taken a minority stake in American space systems contractor Revolution Space.\nThese guys have been around for more than a decade now, and they are in the business of making anything from propulsion systems for spacecraft to the spacecraft (mostly satellites) itself.\nFord's participation in Revolution Space goes beyond simply throwing money at it. We are told the carmaker will apply its manufacturing expertise to scale production of an upcoming spacecraft of an undisclosed nature.\nFrom the few details we got with this announcement, I was able to pick up a few things about this new spacecraft. There is talk of it being able to be part of constellations that do their thing in communications, navigation, and Earth observation.\nThere are hopes the money Ford invests in the space company (a sum that was not disclosed, paid through the carmaker's Industrial Platform LLC subsidiary) will increase Revolution's reach and allow it to place these satellites in low-Earth orbit, geosynchronous orbit, cislunar orbit, and potentially even beyond.\nFord says the move, which came a bit as a surprise, seeing how not that many carmakers turn their attention to the space exploration industry (yet), is part of a broader effort to invest in businesses that have the potential of being very profitable. And this opens the door to potentially other surprise investments in the coming years.\nJust to give us a sense of what profitable means in this case, Ford cites World Economic Forum numbers that point to this industry growing from $600 billion today to $1.8 trillion by 2035.\nMost of that money is to be made in the satellite business, of course. According to Future Market Insights, the satellite market was worth up to $26 billion last year, and should grow to as much as $114 billion over the next decade.\nThere is even a hint that, someday in the future, Ford might make satellites itself. It wouldn't be the first time the carmaker repurposed some of its assembly lines, having already produced bombers, tanks, and jeeps during the Second World War and ventilators and personal protective equipment during the pandemic.\nWhen it comes to space exploration, the Blue Oval has been involved in it before, having produced comms and weather satellites for NASA and the armed forces in the 1960s and 1970s.\nWe'll keep an eye on things and report back when and if we learn anything new about Ford's renewed interest (and involvement) in space exploration.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEditor's note: Gallery shows various Ford images.\nFord Revolution Space satellite\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Chevy Corvette ZR1 Takes On the Lambo Revuelto and the Porsche 911 Turbo S. The Winner Takes It All",
